EXERCISE 5
Clean and transform datasets in Power BI using Power Query by handling duplicates, nulls, and columns
Objective
To prepare clean data for analysis.
Procedure
Step 1: Load Data into Power BI
Open Power BI Desktop
Click Home → Get Data
Select the data source (Excel, CSV, ODS, etc.)
Click Transform Data to open Power Query Editor
Step 2: Remove Duplicate Records
Select the column(s) that should be unique (e.g., Order ID)
Go to Home → Remove Rows → Remove Duplicates
Power Query keeps the first occurrence and removes others
Step 3: Replace or Remove Null Values
Option A: Replace Nulls
Select the column
Go to Transform → Replace Values
Replace null with:
0 for numeric columns
"Unknown" or "Not Available" for text columns
Option B: Remove Null Rows
Select the column
Go to Home → Remove Rows → Remove Blank Rows
Step 4: Rename and Format Columns
Double-click the column header to rename
(e.g., Order_Date → Order Date)
Ensure consistent naming:
No special characters
Use readable names
Adjust text formatting:
Ttrim spaces: Transform → Format → Trim

Step 5: Change Data Types
Select each column
Choose the correct data type from the icon in the column header:
Date → Date
Sales → Decimal Number
Quantity → Whole Number
Category → Text
Confirm changes when prompted
Expected Output
Duplicate records removed
Null values handled appropriately
Columns renamed and formatted consistently
Correct data types applied






 




Result
 Cleaned and transformed dataset ready for analysis and visualization


EXERCISE 6
Create relationships and simple data models in Power BI to understand table connections
Objective
To establish meaningful relationships between multiple tables in Power BI in order to enable accurate analysis and reporting.
Procedure
Step 1: Load Multiple Tables into Power BI
Open Power BI Desktop.
Click Home → Get Data.
Select the data source (Excel / CSV / Database).
Choose multiple related tables (example: Sales, Customers, Products).
Click Load (or Transform Data if cleaning is required).
Step 2: Identify Common Keys
Common keys are columns used to connect tables.
Examples:
Table 1
Common Key
Table 2
Sales
CustomerID
Customers
Orders
OrderID
OrderDetails

Steps:
Go to Data View.
Open each table and inspect column names.
Identify columns with:
Same data type
Same values
Unique values in at least one table (Primary Key)
Example:
Customers[CustomerID] → Primary Key
Sales[CustomerID] → Foreign Key




Step 3: Open Model View
Click the Model View icon (diagram symbol) on the left panel.
You will see all tables displayed as boxes.
Each box contains column names.
Step 4: Create Relationships
Method 1: Drag and Drop
Click and drag the key column from one table.
Drop it onto the matching column in the related table.
Power BI automatically creates a relationship.
Method 2: Manage Relationships
Go to Home → Manage Relationships.
Click New.
Select:
First Table and Column
Second Table and Column
Click OK.
Step 5: Validate Relationship Properties
Double-click the relationship line to verify:
1. Cardinality
One-to-Many (1:*) → Most common
(Example: Customers → Sales)
One-to-One (1:1)
Many-to-Many (:) (use cautiously)
2. Cross Filter Direction
Single (Recommended for beginners)
Both (Use only when required)
3. Active Relationship
Ensure the checkbox “Make this relationship active” is selected.
Click OK after verification.


Step 6: Arrange and Optimize the Data Model
Drag tables to organize them logically.
Place fact tables (Sales, Orders) in the center.
Place dimension tables (Customers, Products, Date) around them.
Remove unnecessary relationships to avoid ambiguity.
Step 7: Validate the Data Model
Go to Report View.
Create a simple visual:
Add Customer Name from Customers
Add Total Sales from Sales
Verify:
Correct aggregation
No blank or incorrect results
Expected Output
A functional data model with properly connected tables
Correct One-to-Many relationships
Clean and understandable Model View layout
Accurate data interaction across visuals


Result
The data model is created successfully with correct table relationships and verified data interactions.

EXERCISE 7
Use DAX to create calculated columns, measures, and perform basic aggregations in Power BI
Objective
To perform calculations in Power BI using Data Analysis Expressions (DAX) by creating measures and calculated columns.
Procedure
Step 1: Open Power BI Report
Open Power BI Desktop.
Load the dataset containing Sales, Cost, Date columns.
Ensure the tables are properly related in Model View.
Step 2: Create Total Sales Measure
Go to Report View.
In the Fields pane, select the Sales table.
Click Modeling → New Measure.
Enter the following DAX formula:
                                            Total Sales = SUM(Sales[SalesAmount])
Press Enter.
Step 3: Create Total Profit Measure
Click Modeling → New Measure.
Enter the DAX formula:
                                           Total Profit = SUM(Sales[SalesAmount]) - SUM(Sales[Cost])
Press Enter.
Step 4: Create Calculated Year Column
Go to Data View.
Select the Sales table.
Click Modeling → New Column.
Enter the DAX formula:
                                            Year = YEAR(Sales[OrderDate])
Press Enter.

Step 5: Validate DAX Calculations
Insert a Table visual.
Add Year, Total Sales, and Total Profit.
Verify that values change correctly when filters are applied.
Expected Output
Correct Total Sales and Total Profit measures
Successfully created Year calculated column
DAX calculations working dynamically with filters


Result
DAX measures and calculated columns are created and validated successfully.

